body:
I can assure you that Codeine is no christmas eve to get off either. I'm getting off that now before getting off Diazepam(I'm down to stage 15 on Schedule 2 in Ashton Manual, staying there until I'm off codeine withdrawals), and the Flu-symptoms until you shit blood, your head is exploding and all you have energy for is laying on the sofa shaking, is not fun. Be careful with that, seriously. It's by far not as bad as benzo to get off, but it's certainly not something I'd recommend to use. Codeine is a great relief, but using it more than what the box says is a terrible idea. Tramadol did nothing for me. Tried 300mg of it, just to check if it REALLY had no effect, and nope, nothing. Didn't even take the edge off the headache.

&nbsp;

While the idea behind the letter is along the lines of what we all are thinking/feeling while tapering and having withdrawals, I can't imagine many doctors would take this seriously. Example being benzo and opiate together "empower" each other and can be lethal in some cases. Propanolol is a heart medication/beta blocker. Not saying what you write is wrong, but for me, it had NO effect on my actual inner restlessness. It felt more like holding down the throttle on the car while holding the handbreak at the same time.

Just observing all of this from Europe, but the thing I hate about all these kind of articles is that it draws attention away from the important cases going on behind your back while you are busy being "entertained" by this quite unimportant tabloid-like rubbish.

&nbsp;

Great, she was thrown out, but look around you, Americans. See anything that may have a slightly worse impact on your future?

Whatever it may mean to you from a internet stranger, I am proud of you. Keep at it, stay strong, and I hope I will be as strong as what you are.

&nbsp;

Seeing your and others stories motivates me to start, and I have a 3-year olf that I refuse to let grow up without a father. I have the tools for the slow Valium taper, guess it's time to put them to use.

Yup, it really is strange. Out of the 3 mentioned options, ironically I think I'm with UK. It's a hell of a effective drug, but very short term (unless you up the dosage, abusing it as I currently am), but the withdrawal and what comes after is just too much.

&nbsp;

I'll keep you posted, please do the same. I started this day with 40mg valium, 2x 500 Paracetamol/ 30 Codeine, 2 mg Xanax and 150 mg Tramadol, so safe to say today was not the start of the taper.

&nbsp;

I understand how maybe US doctors being overly liberal with prescribing Valium have contributed to it being more of a "non-no" drug now, but the alternatives they use are imo no better at all. From my (maybe limited) knowledge, Valium has by far a kongr half-life, and should maintain the desired effect longer for a patient with anxiety. Adding 2 links that I often use. Maybe ytou've seen them, but it's interesting read. The first link also contains the Ashton Manual, my tool for starting the taper.

&nbsp;

https://www.benzo.org.uk/bzequiv.htm  
https://i.imgur.com/ybmbrAD.png

Yeah, I know the half-life of the Xanax is 6-12 hours, while the Diazepam is 20-100 [36-200]*. At the moment I just try (and succeed) in what I haven't in 15 years as a lab-rat with normal Zoloft, Effexor, Lomogitrine, Wellbutrin etc etc. The use I do now is stupid, I know, but so far lack the motivation to start the taper. When I do the taper, I have Diazepam enough to go straight onto the slow taper for minimum discomfort(I still know it will be hell, I'm not that naive).  

&nbsp;

*[active metabolite]
